    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To inspect your outgoing request, you can use browser developer tools or a tool like Postman which allows you to view the full request body prior to sending it. If using the browser, under the \"Network\" tab, look for the request made to your payment gateway API, and click on it to examine \"Headers\" and \"Payload\". Ensure your headers, especially the 'Authorization' header, are formatted correctly with the correct access token. For Postman, just click on the 'Body' tab to see the payload before sending.\n\nAdditionally, make sure your payload format matches the expected structure. Some APIs expect flat JSON, while others may use nested JSON objects. If you’re still encountering issues, consult the API documentation for any additional hidden requirements such as request timeouts, or mandatory headers that might be required by the payment gateway for more secure transactions. Make sure all required fields are present and valid according to the gateway's specification. In some cases, a deeper investigation like enabling verbose logging in your own application or waiting for a response could also give clues to which part of the payload might be failing.",
