    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i tried checking postman and the payload looks perfectly clean, exact same thing as the docs. it's like the server is seeing something completely different than what i'm sending. could there be a middleware or some weird interceptor in the backend that's messing with the data? how would i even track down something that's acting like a hidden glitch or a \"ghost\" in the machine?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To tackle any potential middleware or interceptor issues that's altering your request, first, confirm that there's no custom middleware in the client-side code or the backend server that's incorrectly processing the request payload. If your auth token needs encryption, ensure it's being correctly implemented on the client and decrypted on the server.\n\nIf there's a suspicion of an unexpected intercept before it reaches the payment server, this issue might occur in any transporter code (intermediate proxy services) or gateways since your request only pertains to the payment processing service.\n\nIf you don't have control over the server, contact their support for known issues, or check forums for such problems. Meanwhile, you should enable detailed logging on any transport layers you do control. In your client code, log all payloads before sending to ensure they remain intact; if using code for sending, use a try/catch block to catch payload changes or unexpected errors.\n\nIf you've exhausted all possibilities with your current setup without success, then focusing on the downstream server or middleware may be necessary—working with network team or deployment managers for auditing at the network level could also reveal unexpected behavior like device filtering or manipulations.",
